            "activityType": "Integrated guided explanation plus independent changed problem",
            "purpose": "Embeds the needed Ohm's law and series-circuit explanation, then produces the single piece of independent evidence the accepted outcome requires.",
            "instructions": "Work through this as one sitting.\n\nPart A - Build the idea (about 10 minutes).\n1. Write Ohm's law as V = I x R and state what each symbol means and its unit (volts, amperes, ohms).\n2. For a simple series circuit with a source voltage V_s and two resistors R1 and R2 in one loop, write down the two series rules: the same current I flows through every component, and the total resistance is R_total = R1 + R2.\n3. Combine them: I = V_s / (R1 + R2). Then the voltage across each resistor is V1 = I x R1 and V2 = I x R2, and V1 + V2 = V_s.\n4. Check your understanding with one example of your own choosing, for instance V_s = 9 V, R1 = 100 ohm, R2 = 200 ohm: R_total = 300 ohm, I = 0.03 A, V1 = 3 V, V2 = 6 V, and 3 V + 6 V = 9 V. If you prefer, make up your own numbers instead.\n\nPart B - Independent changed circuit (about 25-35 minutes).\n5. Choose a different simple series circuit with a source voltage and two (or three) resistors in a single loop. Use values you have not used above. Do not look back at Part A while solving; work from the rules you wrote.\n6. For your chosen circuit, calculate and clearly label: the total resistance, the current through the loop, and the voltage across each resistor. Show the formula you use at each step (V = I x R, R_total = sum of resistances).\n7. Verify your answer independently: check that the resistor voltages add up to the source voltage, and check at least one value by substituting back into V = I x R.\n8. Write a short explanation (4-6 sentences) of why the current is the same everywhere in the loop and why the voltages divide in proportion to the resistances.\n\nIf any step does not work out, write down exactly where it broke and what you tried, then correct it before finishing. Finishing this correctly is the stopping point; no further practice set is required.",
