            "instructions": "Create a small CSV file named expenses.csv in the same folder as your Python script. Give it a header row and at least six data rows with three columns: date,category,amount. Use simple values such as 2026-10-01,food,12.50 and 2026-10-02,travel,30. Include exactly one deliberately malformed row, for example a row whose amount is the word 'abc' or that has only two fields, so you can practise handling it. Then write a Python script that: (1) opens expenses.csv and reads it with csv.reader; (2) treats the first row as the header and uses the column positions for category and amount; (3) for each data row, converts the amount text to a number with float() and adds it to a dictionary total keyed by category; (4) wraps the per-row conversion in try/except so that a malformed row is skipped and a clear message is printed instead of crashing the program; (5) after all rows are processed, prints each category and its total, plus a grand total. Run the script and keep the printed output. If the program crashes, read the error message, fix the line it points to, and run again until it prints the grouped totals and a message about the skipped malformed row. You may use any Python reference or documentation you like while writing this first version, but do not copy a complete solution without typing and running it yourself.",
            "completionCriterion": "Your script runs on your own expenses.csv, prints the correct total for each category, prints a grand total, and prints a clear message for the one malformed row without crashing. You can explain in your own words how csv.reader gave you each row, how the amount was converted to a number, how the dictionary accumulated totals, and how try/except skipped the bad row.",
            "effort": {
              "lowMinutes": 40,
              "likelyMinutes": 60,
              "highMinutes": 90
            },
            "timeboxMinutes": 90,
            "commitment": {
              "category": "COMMITTED"
            },
            "hardDependencies": [],
            "splitPoints": [
              {
                "afterMinutes": 45,
                "completionCriterion": "The script reads your CSV with csv.reader and prints each raw row correctly."
              }
            ]
          }
        ],
        "decisions": {
          "assumptions": [
            "You have Python installed and can run a .py file from a terminal or editor.",
            "You can create and edit a small text/CSV file."
          ],
          "tradeOffs": [
            "The first checkpoint uses one small integrated activity instead of separate lessons for CSV reading, grouping and error handling, so you reach a working report quickly; deeper validation, larger datasets and tooling are deliberately left for later."
          ],
          "unresolvedUncertainty": [
            "Whether the one malformed row you create is enough to make you comfortable with error handling; if not, add a second malformed row yourself after the first run.",
            "How much Python you already know beyond the stated gap in CSV reading."
          ],
          "stoppingConditions": [
            "Stop when the script prints correct per-category totals and a grand total from your own CSV and clearly skips the malformed row.",
            "Do not start larger datasets, multiple files, database storage or a full CLI until this checkpoint is independently demonstrated and you explicitly ask for the next milestone."
          ]
        }
      }
    }
  ],
