          "unresolvedUncertainty": [
            "The course's taught method repertoire is unknown, so the difficulty and exact method mix of the five circuits cannot be fixed in advance.",
            "Whether the previous 4-of-5 failure reflects a method-selection gap, a procedural error, or both is not yet diagnosed."
          ],
          "stoppingConditions": [
            "Stop when at least four of five changed circuits are solved correctly with valid method justification and independent checks, and any actual errors are diagnosed and corrected.",
            "If the integrated task succeeds cleanly, do not perform the contingent repair."
          ]
        }
      }
    }
  ],
  "sharedDependencies": [],
  "tradeOffExplanation": "Because your self-reported mastery contradicts your observed 4-of-5 failure, the plan does not certify mastery and instead books one integrated diagnostic-and-repair task on changed circuits within your actually taught methods. This is the smallest useful strategy: it directly tests independent method selection and solution, and it avoids inventing a taught repertoire or a sample data set that were not supplied. A targeted repair is contingent on a concrete failure in that task, so no unnecessary preparation is imposed if the integrated task succeeds. The trade-off is that the exact circuits and methods cannot be pre-specified without knowing your course's taught repertoire; the task therefore requires you to choose changed course-appropriate problems yourself, and the stopping point is a demonstrated 4-of-5 correct independent performance rather than time spent or confidence."
}

